Rostov schrieb:
> 
> So on the later side of a sunny afternoon here in Mountain Standard
> Time, I paid a visit to Fort Ludios to pick up a few things. While I
> was there, a gremlin got generated, and turned into about two dozen
> gremlins. Figuring I better kill them before I came back in the dark,
> and comfortably attired at -22 AC with a +6 Frostbrand, my Ranger
> strode fearlessly to meet them.
> 
> "The gremlin chuckles. You feel vulnerable."
> 
> What? Oh. The alt.org server, on which I am playing, is probably in a
> different time zone. O-kay. Yet Another Annoying Detail To Beware Of,
> but not fatal. Besides, I've got all the gold I used to buy my
> protection (most of which I purchased from Moloch's Priest in the
> Valley of the Dead, who I subsequently had killed by a Rock Troll) and
> more. I'll just pop on over to the Minetown or to the Temple on Level
> 16 and buy it all back, right?
> 
> "Thy selfless generosity is greatly appreciated."
> 
> What? I clearly don't have any protection. The gremlin's attack took
> ALL of it.

Yes and no. You no longer enjoy the benefits of protection, but you
can get _all_ of it back with _one_ successful action.

The problem is that getting protection nuked by angering <yourdeity>
and falling victim to gremlin theft are two _totally_ different
things:

Having the intrinsic stolen by a gremlin leaves the amount intact, but
turns the intrinsic off. 

Angering your deity sets your protection _amount_ to zero - you still
have the intrinsic "you are protected".

If you will, in the first case, you're _not protected_ for (e.g.) ten
points, in the second you're protected for _zero_ points.[1]

The problem is that you'll only get the intrinsic back if you succeed
in a roll to _increase_ protection, which is likely to fail (and to
keep failing) for buying when the amount is 9+. If you have a
convenient co-aligned altar and a source for monster generation,
sacrificing and praying should eventually trick your deity into
"granting thee the gift of <deity's> protection".
In both cases, such granting of protection will in fact _not_ increase
the amount but simply give you the intrinsic back - for the full
amount.

I'm not sure what happens if you get protection stolen and _then_
anger your deity, i.e. if the amount of the stolen intrinsic will be
set to zero: that could be a desparate measure to _buy_ protection
back at a temple.

[1] Obviously, that seems to be an unnecessarily fine distinction when
you have a naked AC of 10 (in original shape) in either case, but it
makes a difference when trying to get the naked AC back down again.

